# Reusability Patterns in Action

1. Shared Configurations: 
Use <default_args_config_path or default_args_config_dict> to define common task settings — retry counts, email alerts, timeout values — once, and apply them globally. Changes propagate instantly to all DAGs that reference them.

2. Modular YAML Definitions
Large DAGs can be split into:
  ingestion.yaml
  transform.yaml
  quality.yaml
This improves maintainability.

3. Dynamic Task Mapping
Very important modern Airflow feature.
Instead of manually creating tasks:
  task1
  task2
  task3
Airflow dynamically generates tasks at runtime.

# Templating for Consistency
This is enterprise standardization.
One template can power:
  hundreds of DAGs
  while ensuring:
    same retry policies
    same naming standards
    same monitoring

# Loading DAGs from a Folder
one of DAG Factory's biggest features.[One of DAG Factory's most powerful features is its ability to automatically discover and register every YAML DAG definition within a specified directory. Drop a new YAML file into the folder and it becomes a live Airflow DAG — no manual registration required.]

Traditional Airflow
Normally: every DAG must exist in Python file

DAG Factory
You only place YAML inside folder.
Example:
  from dagfactory import load_yaml_dags
  load_yaml_dags(
      globals_dict=globals(),
      dags_folder="/dags"
  )

What Happens?
DAG Factory:
  Scans folder
  Finds YAML files
  Generates DAGs
  Registers automatically

Huge Enterprise Advantage
Adding DAG becomes:
  Drop YAML file → DAG appears in Airflow
No manual coding needed. 

# CI/CD Integration
This is VERY important.
Developers:
